<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elizabeth I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1533">1533</a>–<a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Elizabeth I of England · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">House of Tudor</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elizabeth I was Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Ireland from 1558 to 1603, the last Tudor monarch. Her religious settlement established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and her reign saw the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-armada">Spanish Armada</a> in 1588, early English overseas ventures and a flowering of literature. In Ireland her forces crushed the Desmond rebellions and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i.jpg" alt="Elizabeth I" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darnley_stage_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rebels offered sovereignty first to Francis, Duke of Anjou, and, after William the Silent was assassinated at Delft in 1584, to <b>Elizabeth I of England</b>, who declined it but sent troops under the Earl of Leicester.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Territorial states grew stronger and less willing to tolerate foreign privileges: Ivan III of Moscow closed the Novgorod office in 1494, Sweden under Gustav Vasa and Denmark reduced Lübeck’s influence after the Count’s Feud, and <b>Elizabeth I of England</b> revoked the privileges of the London Steelyard in 1598.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Elizabeth I</b> died childless in 1603, her cousin James VI of Scotland became James I of England, uniting the two crowns while leaving two separate kingdoms with their own parliaments, churches and laws.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The two had shared a monarch since 1603, when James VI of Scotland succeeded <b>Elizabeth I</b> as James I of England, but they kept separate parliaments, churches, laws and trade, and a union imposed under Oliver Cromwell had lapsed at the Restoration.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In foreign policy he balanced between Spain and the Ottoman Empire and opened relations with England: he exchanged letters with <b>Elizabeth I</b>, and in 1600 his envoy Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud visited London to propose an alliance against Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…with Rome to Protestant reform under Edward VI, to Mary I’s restoration of Catholicism, under which more than 280 Protestants were burned, and to the Protestant settlement of <b>Elizabeth I</b>, whose government executed more than a hundred Catholic priests, and some lay people who sheltered them, as traitors.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palace of Placentia, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Richmond Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-0.01&amp;year=1578" aria-label="Palace of Placentia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4550 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Elizabeth I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5000 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5000 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.48,-0.01&amp;year=1578">What was here in 1578? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I know I have the body but of a weak and feeble woman; but I have the heart and stomach of a king…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the troops at Tilbury, 1588 &#183; original &#183; as recorded by Leonel Sharp, printed in 1654 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Speech_to_the_Troops_at_Tilbury" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no jewel, be it of never so rich a price, which I set before this jewel: I mean your love.&#8221;</p><footer>The Golden Speech to Parliament, 1601 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elizabeth_I%27s_Farewell_Speech" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I grieve and dare not show my discontent, I love and yet am forced to seem to hate, I do, yet dare not say I ever meant, I seem stark mute but inwardly do prate.&#8221;</p><footer>On Monsieur's Departure (poem) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/On_Monsieur%27s_Departure" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To be a king and wear a crown is a thing more glorious to them that see it than it is pleasant to them that bear it.&#8221;</p><footer>The Golden Speech to Parliament, 1601 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elizabeth_I%27s_Farewell_Speech" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I never was any greedy, scraping grasper, nor a strait fast-holding Prince, nor yet a waster. My heart was never set on any worldly goods.&#8221;</p><footer>The Golden Speech to Parliament, 1601 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Elizabeth_I%27s_Farewell_Speech" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I do not desire to live to distrust my faithful and loving people. Let tyrants fear, I have always so behaved myself that, under God, I have placed my chiefest strength and safeguard in the loyal hearts and good-will of my subjects…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech to the troops at Tilbury, 1588 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Speech_to_the_Troops_at_Tilbury" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
